{
  "rules": [
    "Near-term (t+1 to t+7): model is slightly high on average (~+0.1 to +0.4). Apply a small downward bias correction in the first week.",
    "Mid-horizon (t+8 to t+22): model increasingly overpredicts during drawdowns/mean-reversion phases (often +0.5 to +1.5). If the recent 5–10 day slope is negative, damp forecasts toward the recent mean (e.g., shrink deviations from 10–20d average by ~20–40%).",
    "Late horizon (t+23 to t+30): model fails to capture upside breakouts after consolidation and becomes too low (can be -2 to -5). If spot is rising and/or breaks above the prior 10–15d high, add an upward drift term that increases with horizon (e.g., +0.5 by t+25 ramping to +2 to +4 by t+30).",
    "Spike handling: after sharp up-moves, the model tends to extrapolate too much for ~1–2 weeks (overshoot). Cap week-2 forecast gains and enforce partial mean reversion unless price is making new highs.",
    "Regime-switch heuristic: use two modes—(A) 'reversion' when price is below/near 20d mean and momentum is flat/negative (apply stronger downward/damping), (B) 'trend' when price is above 20d mean and momentum positive (reduce damping and allow positive drift, especially beyond t+20)."
  ]
}
